# modules.cpp audio interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.x specifications. This document specifies the
implemented portable audio interface and its microphone input and ES8311 codec
output drivers. mm.audio, both drivers, and their host tests exist. Audio
behaviour on hardware remains a hardware-qualification requirement rather than
a completed claim.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented mm.mcu
interface, including the ADC, I2C, and I2S facilities these drivers use.
docs/modules-mm.mdy remains authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

# Decision

Audio is a device service layered above mm.mcu ADC, I2C, and I2S. It is not a
mixer, an effect engine, a resampler, and not a policy module.

The intended dependency direction is:

Opening fence
portable application
        |
     mm.audio
        |
selected board audio provider
        |
mm.audio.microphone (input) and mm.audio.es8311 (output)
        |
ADC, I2C, and I2S from mm.mcu
        |
selected MCU platform provider
Closing fence

An application using only portable audio behavior declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.audio
Closing fence

It does not name a controller, vendor, I2C address, ADC channel, GPIO number,
or provider module. The selected physical board profile supplies those facts.

# Portable interface

mm.audio is a platform interface with an allocation-free API for description,
initialization, capture, playback, and shutdown. Its fallback objects report
Unsupported.

The public shape is:

Opening fence
enum class Status { Ok, BadArgument, Unsupported,
                    NotInitialized, Busy, Timeout, TransportError };

using Sample = std::int16_t;

enum class Direction { Input, Output };

struct Description {
    std::string_view name = "";
    unsigned int rate = 0;
    unsigned int channels = 0;
    Direction direction = Direction::Input;
};

class Microphone {
public:
    virtual ~Microphone() = default;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Description description() const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status capture(std::span<Sample>, std::size_t& count);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status shutdown();
};

class Codec {
public:
    virtual ~Codec() = default;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Description description() const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status play(std::span<const Sample>);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status shutdown();
};

void set_microphone(Microphone&);
[[nodiscard]] Microphone& selected_microphone();
void set_codec(Codec&);
[[nodiscard]] Codec& selected_codec();
Closing fence

`capture` fills the caller's span with as many samples as the microphone draws,
and sets `count` to the number captured. `count` is written only when the call
answers Ok, so a caller that ignores the status cannot mistake a stale count
for a fresh reading. A span of any size is a valid capture; nothing audible is
not an error.

`play` ships the caller's samples to the codec. There is no count out, because
a codec either takes the frame it was given or it does not.

`Description` names the device, the rate it samples at, how many channels it
carries, and which direction it faces. The two ends of the link are separate
classes because they are separate devices: a microphone a caller captures from,
and a codec a caller plays to. A board that has one but not the other still
composes, and a caller that wants both names them.

Like mm.touch, a device is polled: a caller calls capture or play, and a
provider may use its own interrupt or DMA internally to make that cheap. Exposing
an interrupt or DMA descriptor would mean exposing a callback registration
mm.mcu does not have.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board's audio provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.audio.

# Microphone driver

mm.audio.microphone implements a microphone input digitised by the platform's
ADC. It contains no vendor header and no board pin number: a provider supplies
the channel and this module drives the protocol.

The driver receives immutable wiring:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    unsigned int channel;   // the ADC channel the microphone's output is on
    unsigned int rate;      // the sample rate the caller captures at
    unsigned int bits;      // the ADC's width, for the count-to-sample scale
};
Closing fence

A microphone is an analog source on an ADC channel. `initialize` claims the
channel through mm.mcu `adc_configure`; the ADC is the authority on whether the
channel exists, and a channel it does not offer answers BadArgument. `capture`
draws one conversion per sample and maps each count to a sixteen-bit sample: the
count is centred on the ADC's half scale, so a quiet input is a sample near zero
rather than a large positive count, then scaled to sixteen bits. `shutdown`
releases the channel.

The ADC's conversion rate bounds real-time capture. That is a
hardware-qualification, as the touch drivers treat their panel behaviour, and
this module does not promise a rate the ADC cannot deliver.

# The ES8311 driver

mm.audio.es8311 implements the EverAs ast ES8311 audio codec, its DAC as a
playback output, over portable mm.mcu I2C and I2S. It takes the same immutable
wiring shape:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::I2cConfiguration i2c;
    unsigned int address;        // the codec's I2C address, per its strap pins
    mm::mcu::I2sConfiguration i2s;
    unsigned int rate;           // the sample rate playback runs at
    unsigned long reset_delay_ms; // the pause between entering and leaving reset
};
Closing fence

Modelled on the mainline Linux ASoC driver for this codec. The codec answers on
an eight-bit register and eight-bit value I2C bus, and is identified by the
address its provider assigns rather than by a chip identifier register, which is
how that driver probes it. `initialize` configures the I2C bus and performs the
driver's reset and power-up sequence: it enters reset mode on the reset register,
pauses for `reset_delay_ms`, leaves reset mode, clears the two system registers
to power up the state machine, and programs the output channel for I2S playback.
It then configures the I2S link. `play` ships the caller's samples down the I2S
link as sixteen-bit little-endian bytes. `shutdown` mutes the output channel.

Two things in the ASoC driver are deliberately not reproduced. Its clock
dividers, which turn the board's master clock into the bit and word clocks, are
absent, because they depend on the master clock a board provides; the I2S link's
baud is what this module controls. Its ADC and microphone path is absent,
because this module is the output end and the input is mm.audio's microphone.

# Provider binding

mm.audio carries the existing platform-interface marker. A concrete hardware
board binds each end of the link to a provider:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.audio platform.<board>.microphone
platform-provider: mm.audio platform.<board>.es8311
Closing fence

A provider imports mm.audio, the selected driver module, and mm.mcu. It owns the
fixed ADC channel, I2C and I2S wiring, and device rate, constructs the driver,
and registers the implementation from a retained static object. Existing provider
validation, board-over-SDK precedence, conditional closure injection, and core
platform-edge rules apply unchanged.

# Test strategy

Driver tests use a fake MCU Platform that records the ADC, I2C, and I2S
operations. Tests compare semantic transcripts rather than merely checking that
a source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- the microphone's count-to-sample centre and scale;
- the conversions a capture draws, one per sample;
- the channel a microphone claims and releases;
- the ES8311's reset and power-up transcript in order;
- the output-channel program for I2S playback;
- the sixteen-bit little-endian frames play ships down the I2S link;
- invalid-wiring refusals and the reinitialization path.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms the audio and MCU provider
registration objects. It then flashes through flash.sh.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.audio with a host stand-in independent of any driver.
2. Implemented: add mm.audio.microphone with capture and channel tests.
3. Implemented: add mm.audio.es8311 with reset, power-up, and playback tests.
4. Pending hardware: qualify capture and playback on a named board with a
   microphone and an ES8311.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.audio links with exactly one selected audio provider
  per end of the link, and the provider's driver closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no audio binding.
- An app not importing mm.audio contains no audio provider or driver
  registration object.
- Driver host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- I2C register accesses cross the platform boundary as write-read transactions,
  not separate write and read calls, and playback crosses as I2S writes.
- No driver source includes vendor or Pico SDK headers.
- A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful capture or
  playback.

# What this does not settle

- Microphone input is digitised by the platform's ADC, and output is the
  ES8311's DAC. The ES8311's ADC and microphone path is absent, because this
  module's input is mm.audio's microphone.
- Mixing, effects, resampling, level policy, and sample-format conversion are
  policy above this module.
- A data-ready interrupt or DMA descriptor is not exposed; mm.mcu has no
  callback registration.
- Multi-device selection and runtime audio discovery are not specified.
